WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.500
- And number two, you have to,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.040
outside of your job, build your skills

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:04.873
in this world.

00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:06.800
Otherwise, you'll be vulnerable.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:07.848
Period.

00:00:07.848 --> 00:00:08.681
(whooshing)

00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:09.747
Good morning.

00:00:09.747 --> 00:00:10.580
(whooshing)

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.413
Really nice to meet you guys.

00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:13.400
I'd love to do as much Q&amp;A as possible.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.240
I'll pick it up from that point.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.470
In 2009, on the back of

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.530
a lot of success early in my life,

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.250
especially by investing in Facebook

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.330
and Twitter, it became
very obvious to me that

00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:30.500
the world was about

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.150
to go through a substantial change

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.070
and that this internet thing was

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.780
far bigger than what I
even thought as a kid

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.240
and that we were just in the beginning.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.073
Not the end.

00:00:39.073 --> 00:00:40.610
And I think sometimes,
especially for you guys,

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:41.940
like, you know how timing is right,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.560
you think it's over.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.730
It's like a band you like.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:45.750
You think it's over but they didn't

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.020
even start getting big,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:47.960
even though you've been on for

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.670
a year and a half, that's kind of

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.880
how I felt about the internet as a whole.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.870
My father-in-law was Jim
Kilts' right hand marketing guy

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.080
at Nabisco, Kraft and Gillette.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.310
And so at family functions, barbecues

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:02.840
and random times, I would hear them talk

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.210
about big brands and they were winning.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.960
They were like considered like winning

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.880
and they were doing all the right things.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.673
Every company had turned around.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.070
And then something very
interesting had happened.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.320
I went on vacation with my wife

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.410
and I don't really read a lot books ever.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.800
Like I've read nine in my life,

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.490
but one of them was about
the private equity firm 3G,

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.590
which went on to buy Budweiser and Kraft

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.110
and Hines and I was reading it

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.090
and just kind of this
series of life is funny.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.160
Sometimes it's about
getting the pieces together

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.150
at the right moment

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.410
and having the time to think

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.640
and really the only time I think

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.810
is on this vacation

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.760
and so I'm kind of walking the beach

00:01:43.694 --> 00:01:45.877
and kind of out like a
central casting movie, I

00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:46.980
was like, fuck, it just all clicked,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.130
which is, huh, if 3G's gonna win,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:50.963
and I think they are,

00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:52.390
and this was in 2009,

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.000
for buying Burger King, firing everybody.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.370
Maximizing profit.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.690
Wall Street's gonna like that.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.480
That made sense to me.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.040
If Facebook and Twitter and YouTube

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.000
are gonna win, television's
gonna get disrupted

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:05.833
even more so.

00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:08.420
And I thought it was already dead

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.723
by commercial's standards.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.610
As we're here this week,

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.140
it's still very much on an
emotional pedestal and so,

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.390
(cheering)

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.260
I basically starting putting things

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.060
together in my mind and saying, wow.

00:02:24.022 --> 00:02:26.760
Private equity, these brands and then I

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.190
really thought Amazon
was a very big company

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.300
at the time and it's now been proven

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.133
to be that,

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.150
and I was like, I think retail

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.170
is in trouble.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.430
My dad, this business
that we're referring to,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.960
my dad had a liquor store in New Jersey.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.420
I launched one of the first
3E commerce wine businesses

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.850
in American and just everything

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.140
just kind of came together.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.780
My brother was graduating college.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.050
We wanted to start a business together

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:54.190
and then ESPN a week later asked me

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.860
to come in an consult and they said we'll

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.300
give you five thousand dollars for an hour

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.690
and I was like, fuck.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.350
So, it was all kind of coming together

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.280
and I was like, you know, there's,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.770
it might be time for me
to leave Silicon Valley.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.710
I'd done the entrepreneur, entrepreneur,

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.000
like real entrepreneur shit.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.900
Not this new tech version, like being

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.220
in a liquor store 15 hours a day

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.713
for 10 years.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.840
I need to figure out this
corporate American thing

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.523
because it's big and it's wrong.

00:03:22.793 --> 00:03:25.020
And knowing that I was a D
and F student, I couldn't

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.880
go MBA it out

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.730
or hire a couple of people.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:29.670
I just don't learn that way.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.790
I was like, let me go eat shit

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.010
for a decade.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.310
I'm gonna build an agency.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:35.840
I didn't even know what that meant.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.150
I called my company VaynerMedia.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.380
We only did creative for
the first four years.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.530
Like, I didn't know that media

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.740
and creative was separate.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.510
When I tell you I knew nothing,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.010
like I'd never heard
of WPP or David Ogleby

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.470
or any, I was 34 years old, starting

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.730
an agency and never heard anything

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.880
that you'll hear the whole day.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:54.880
I didn't know what Canne was.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.310
I didn't know what ad age was.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.080
I didn't know Wieden and Kennedy was.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.310
I knew nothing.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:00.420
Here's what I knew.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.090
I knew that the end consumer
wasn't being communicated

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.060
to properly and that
within the first six months

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.670
and 10 or 15 meetings and
a little bit of reading,

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.930
I knew the whole thing was trading

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.260
on the wrong things.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.680
Here's the amazing
advantages when you don't

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.392
come from the world.

00:04:17.392 --> 00:04:18.730
A couple things.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.090
When you walk into the world

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:21.660
and you find out the Nielsen ratings

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.244
are super important to
justify things, you what

00:04:25.244 --> 00:04:27.990
a new person does, like me,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.110
who's an operator.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.890
You try to figure out how Nielsen ratings

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.800
are being measured.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.170
What you realize very
quickly, is like, holy shit,

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.870
this is mathematically correct

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.540
but not practically correct.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.070
You can't have two thousand boxes

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.010
and people calling people and asking them

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.970
if they watched Charlie Rose last night

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:45.840
to figure out if,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.560
it was absurd.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.500
And the absurdity continued every day.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.510
And so, a couple things

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.390
that I'd like to establish.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:54.880
Number one, I think creative is

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.830
the variable of success for brands.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.620
So, I'm not a mathematician or a quant guy

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.860
or you know, to me, Just Do It.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.130
Where's The Beef, Priceless.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.230
It is the holy grail.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.230
When you produce something that gets

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.206
everybody to know, that's how

00:05:10.206 --> 00:05:12.340
the human brain makes decisions.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:13.810
It's not 'cuz we cookie them

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:15.880
and follow them around and buy.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.820
My problem, though, is I'm
also unbelievably practical.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.620
My whole life has been, my work

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.040
has to put food on the table.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:23.920
Not win awards.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:24.753
Not get headlines.

00:05:24.753 --> 00:05:25.950
Not get me a promotion.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.270
So, I've always cared if it works.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.590
There isn't a commercial today

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.820
that works because nobody's
watching television commercials.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.100
That's just the truth.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.900
And it's a truth me and
the creative community

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.000
have to start accepting because otherwise,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.420
we're gonna have this rub

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:42.670
and otherwise, we're gonna continue

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:43.503
to see what we're seeing,

00:05:43.503 --> 00:05:44.790
which is the biggest brands in the world

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.690
over the last seven years
have declined massively.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:49.610
They're losing market share every day.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.510
And we have to start having
the proper conversation.

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:55.700
And so to me, we're in this
amazing perfect time now

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.400
where it's never been
better to be a creative.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.130
You have unlimited flexibility.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:03.310
It's like being in
production in Hollywood.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.510
It's great now.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.930
Everyone's winning because there's Hulu

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.800
and Amazon Prime and Netflix and HBO and,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.430
there's so many places to sell your work.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.270
Hollywood's figured it out.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.310
They were shitting on
Netflix five years ago.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.180
Now they're like kissing its face

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.380
because it opened up everything.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:21.840
I literally, I'd love for the front row

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.931
to see this.

00:06:22.931 --> 00:06:23.810
I have huge goosebumps right now

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.434
because as I'm saying
it, I'm so passionate

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:27.270
because I love this space.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.770
Because you know, the thing that sucks

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.277
about being in something in
10 years, you meet people

00:06:30.277 --> 00:06:33.250
and you like them and just
like the thing, right?

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.040
I love this industry.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.200
I hate that it hasn't figured out

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.020
that the second they put the romance

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.060
of television away, we will all flourish.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:42.893
I don't know what else to say.

00:06:42.893 --> 00:06:44.780
There is no Just Do It commercial

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:45.613
over the last 10 years.

00:06:45.613 --> 00:06:48.080
There isn't a commercial
that has driven business

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.540
like they used to in the
'70s and '80s and '90s,

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.160
'cuz people don't watch them.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.170
And now on the flip side, Superbowl

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:56.560
is the best piece of
advertising opportunity

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.393
in the world.

00:06:57.393 --> 00:06:58.500
To me, the Superbowl commercial is

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.730
the number one place I would spend money

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.070
on a TV spot.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.370
But it's a singular unit.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.440
Literally, the Oscars and the Tony's

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.930
and the NFC championship game

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.180
and the World Cup, all
that stuff, garbage.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.320
People don't watch it.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.623
It's black and white.

00:07:12.868 --> 00:07:15.360
And so, Hollywood, to their credit,

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.210
quickly got off their bullshit

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:18.990
and said fuck it, because

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:20.280
the economics were there.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.900
When Netflix started writing big checks,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.440
it was fine, right?

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.710
The reason we haven't, is that's

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:25.543
not what happened.

00:07:25.543 --> 00:07:28.000
The reason it hasn't happened in our world

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.150
is 'cuz the holding companies

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.960
aren't incentivized to change.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.060
There's nothing else to say.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:33.510
That's just real life business.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.660
There's more margin in
selling programmatic digital

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.080
and selling television
than any other category.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.360
Thus, that is the continued propaganda

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.080
of our industry to the
demise of creative work.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.930
The fact that you can
make a 17 minute film

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.270
on Facebook that if it was fucking awesome

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:50.930
and you play on the media properly would

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:54.200
really sell and make
people excited is amazing.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.000
You know, the work that I'm most proud of

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.010
at VaynerMedia are all long form videos.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.310
Facebook says don't do
it 'cuz it doesn't work

00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:01.988
for their three second views.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:03.933
You know, the brands don't us to do it.

00:08:03.933 --> 00:08:04.810
Nobody want's us to do it,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.480
but every time we do it, miraculously,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.210
the product sells at supermarkets

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.700
and Walmarts and things of that nature.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.240
And that's even with the
risk of the creative missing.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.880
And it still works because
it's so being consumed

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:18.070
in those environment.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:21.140
Yet unfortunately, and
I have a lot of empathy

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:23.540
for this room, you don't grow up dreaming

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:27.220
about writing one singular
tweet like for Wendy's

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.870
or Moon Pie that can change it's business.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:30.020
It's just not what you're here for.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.820
We're not here in this
sunny fuckin' place, right,

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.830
for a, you know, the pedestal isn't about

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:38.770
a one minute 19 second video that's

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.360
a preroll on YouTube
before something else.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.130
It's just not what's on a pedestal

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:43.963
and it's wrong.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.320
And it's wrong.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.970
And I believe that and
so, I'm very passionate

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.520
to continue to communicate that

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.830
and what really bugs me the most

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:58.090
is that when you think about audio,

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.020
the rise of audio, sonic advertising,

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.410
the fact that every brand should have

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.210
a jingle and a three second sound

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:05.780
that we can associate with.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.795
The fact that you can
make five minute videos.

00:09:08.795 --> 00:09:11.720
The fact that you can make 11 videos

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.780
for a brand instead of one,

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.180
is in theory, amazing for creative.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.360
Like, wouldn't you want to make 11 videos

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:19.255
instead of one?

00:09:19.255 --> 00:09:20.750
Look at this room.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:22.430
Nobody here looks the same.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.500
Shouldn't, if I'm Dove, make one

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.100
for African American females,

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.930
make one for white guys with blonde hair,

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:30.090
make one for psycho-graphic, demographic,

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.350
financial, interests, shouldn't I?

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:33.670
The answer's yes.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:35.850
But the system forces us to do

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.590
a subjective vanilla tag line

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:39.940
that should work for everybody

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.160
and then make one 30 fucking second video

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.520
that should work for
everyone, AKA white people

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.770
and the shit doesn't hit.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.360
And everybody who's smart knows it.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.430
Or has looked up.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:53.880
The biggest vulnerability,

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:55.670
the reason, if I'm talking right now,

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:56.700
everybody's like huh,

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.490
is you just haven't looked up.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:09:59.420
